{"description":"Unify the `eka mcp` command family entrypoints. Today `eka mcp` and `eka mcp serve` BOTH start the MCP server, and both emit human-readable output - which is wrong on two counts: (1) the bare command gives no guided path to discover/install skills and commands per agent, and (2) human-readable output on a stdio transport risks corrupting the JSON-RPC stream that agents consume.\n\nTarget behavior:\n- `eka mcp` (bare): informational output about eka mcp plus an INTERACTIVE installer for skills/commands - modern TUI via bubbletea. Flow must include a step to pick the target agent and a step to pick the target config scope (global user-level vs repository-level).\n- `eka mcp serve`: runs the MCP server silently (protocol-only stdio, no banner/human output). This becomes THE command referenced from per-agent MCP configurations.\n- Migration: per-agent configs currently invoke the eka-mcp plugin binary directly; they should move to `eka mcp serve` so the CLI owns the entrypoint (versioning, path resolution, updates) instead of a loose plugin binary.","acceptanceCriteria":"- Bare `eka mcp` NEVER starts the server: it prints an informational overview of eka mcp (what it is, current install/config status) and launches an interactive installer for skills/commands.\n- Interactive installer built on bubbletea (charmbracelet) for a modern terminal UI, with at minimum two steps: (1) pick target agent, (2) pick target config scope - global (user-level) or repository-level.\n- Non-TTY fallback defined and implemented (flags or plain mode) so CI and agent runners are never blocked by the interactive surface.\n- `eka mcp serve` runs the MCP server with ZERO human-readable output on stdout - stdout carries JSON-RPC protocol traffic only; diagnostics go to stderr. Stdout purity is protocol-critical for stdio transports.\n- Per-agent MCP configurations migrated from invoking the eka-mcp binary directly to `eka mcp serve`; migration path documented, and the interactive installer offers to update existing configs.\n- Tests cover: serve stdout purity (no non-protocol bytes), non-TTY fallback behavior, and installer step logic.\n- Knowledge stays project-agnostic: no references to other projects' codebases in CKO content; external references live in review discussion only."}